diff --git a/docs/playing.md b/docs/playing.md
index cda980d3..9d7757d0 100644
--- a/docs/playing.md
+++ b/docs/playing.md
@@ -44,7 +44,7 @@ A Mac app shows the game's settings in a window before the game starts ([The Mac
 
 A controller macOS pairs but Apple's controller support does not list, such as an 8BitDo in its own mode (Ultimate 2C Wireless over Bluetooth), is read as a raw HID gamepad, with full analog sticks: it takes a player as any other controller does, and the log says `controller '<name>' (HID) connected`. A pad Apple's support says it will list gets two seconds to appear there first, so it is never read twice (an Xbox, PlayStation or Nintendo controller is always left to Apple's support); if it never appears, it is read as HID after that wait, and the log says so. Its buttons follow 8BitDo's numbering by default; another maker's pad may number them differently, and the log then says so. `DM_HID_MAP=A=1,B=2,X=4,Y=5` in `env.txt` moves them (here: the printed letters instead of the positions), `DM_HID_LOG=1` logs the number of each button pressed, and `DM_HID_PADS=0` turns this off.
 
-Two identical controllers that Apple's controller support lists as one, such as two Pro Controllers over USB, are each a player of their own: after 3 seconds the log says `GameController lists 1 of 2`, and each takes a player, by USB port. They have no vibration, player lights or Home button to quit. `DM_PAD_IDENTICAL=0` in `env.txt` turns this off.
+Two identical controllers that Apple's controller support lists as one, such as two Pro Controllers over USB, are each a player of their own: after 3 seconds the log says `GameController lists 1 of 2`, and each takes a player, by USB port. Pro Controllers read this way have no vibration, player lights or Home button to quit. A pad read from Nintendo report `0x3F` instead gets player lights and can quit with Home. `DM_PAD_IDENTICAL=0` in `env.txt` turns this off.
 
 With [Press a button to join](settings.md#press-a-button-to-join) on in the settings (`DM_PAD_PRESS_TO_JOIN=1` in `env.txt`), on the Mac and on iOS, a controller becomes a player only when one of its buttons is pressed, as the lowest player free: the players choose their order and how many play, over Bluetooth or USB, identical pads included, and a controller connected while the game runs joins at its first press. The log says `press a button to play`, then `a button pressed: player N`. The press that joins also reaches the game. When a game asks for more players than it sees, a prompt over the game says how many are wanted and joined and waits for the others: a controller that presses a button (or connects, with the setting off) joins, A finishes once enough are in, and B or Escape closes it, as on a Switch.
 
diff --git a/docs/reference/services.md b/docs/reference/services.md
index 07fd066f..c8be5b1c 100644
--- a/docs/reference/services.md
+++ b/docs/reference/services.md
@@ -403,7 +403,7 @@ Most service calls can be found before they crash, before the first boot or the
 - **Cause:** GameController lists one controller for both. Their USB serial numbers are the same, which may be why; over Bluetooth each has its own. IOKit has both devices, and each sends its report `0x30`. GameController gives a controller no HID identity, so its one controller cannot be tied to one of the two devices.
 - **Fix:** one mechanism in `pad_hid_mac.h`, on its own HID manager. It keeps the devices GameController reads (`g_hidpads_gc`) instead of dropping them, to count IOKit's pads of each model (vendor and product ID) against GameController's controllers of it: a Pro Controller's by their product category, another model's by name (`vendorName` is the HID product string). Other Microsoft, Sony and Nintendo pads cannot be matched by name (a DualShock 4's HID product string is `Wireless Controller`), so they are never read this way, which would read them twice. When IOKit has at least two pads of a model and more than GameController lists, after a 3 s wait for a pad that is not listed yet (`pad_dupes_want`), every pad of that model is read there, a player each, by USB location: a Pro Controller from its report `0x30` (`pad_pro.h`) with its gyro (`motion_pro.h`), any other model from its HID elements (`pad_hid.h`). `front_mac.m` leaves GameController's controllers of that model unread (`hidpad_gc_unread`). When GameController lists them all again, they go back to it. `DM_PAD_IDENTICAL=0` turns it off, as does `DM_HID_PADS=0`; `DM_MOTION=0` and `DM_MOTION_HID=0` turn off only the gyro. `motion_pro_mac.h` is not involved: it still pairs one Pro Controller's raw motion with GameController's one controller.
 - **Sticks:** a Pro Controller read this way asks for its stick calibration in SPI flash, subcommand `0x10` in output report `0x01`: the user's at `0x8010` when its magic `B2 A1` is there, else the factory's at `0x603D` ([spi_flash_notes.md](https://github.com/dekuNukem/Nintendo_Switch_Reverse_Engineering/blob/master/spi_flash_notes.md)). Alongside the macOS driver both pads answered over USB, and a pad over Bluetooth, on an M4 Pro with macOS 27. Without an answer each axis measures its own rest and reach.
-- **Limits:** such pads have no rumble or player lights, and Home does not quit from them. Another model's buttons follow 8BitDo's numbering unless `DM_HID_MAP` says otherwise; only Pro Controllers were run, before the mechanism was folded into `pad_hid_mac.h` ([PR #1595](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1595)), and the folded version awaits a run with two Pro Controllers over USB.
+- **Limits:** such pads have no rumble. Pro Controllers read from report `0x30` have no player lights, and Home does not quit from them. Report `0x3F` pads get player lights and quit with Home. Another model's buttons follow 8BitDo's numbering unless `DM_HID_MAP` says otherwise; only Pro Controllers were run, before the mechanism was folded into `pad_hid_mac.h` ([PR #1595](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/1595)), and the folded version awaits a run with two Pro Controllers over USB.
 - **Found on:** [Mario Kart World](../../dm/games/0400C3F00006E000/README.md) 1.8.0, two Pro Controllers over USB, 33 minutes of two-player split-screen races.
 
 ### One Pro Controller read as two players
@@ -533,7 +533,7 @@ Most service calls can be found before they crash, before the first boot or the
 
 - **Symptom:** on the Mac, a Pro Controller clone read as a raw HID pad (a PowerA NANO pairs over Bluetooth as `Lic Pro Controller`, HID vendor and product 0) has its buttons in the wrong places, a full stick push does not run (it reads 61 to 74%), and its four lights keep running in a chase.
 - **Cause:** such a pad's report descriptor is Nintendo's, and it stays in the Pro Controller's simple report `0x3F`. That report numbers its buttons differently from the default 8BitDo map, carries its sticks raw (the NANO's rest 9 and 11% off centre and reach about 0.78 of the axis from there), and the pad chases its lights until a console gives it a player.
-- **Fix (resolved):** buttons that come in report `0x3F` take that report's numbering, read from the device's elements and not from its name or vendor. `pad_hid_raw_axis` measures each of its axes from its first value and scales it by the reach seen so far, which starts at 0.7 and only grows: a full push reads 99 to 100%. The pad gets its player on its lights (`pad_hid_lights`: subcommand `0x30` of output report `0x01`; the NANO answers `80 30` in report `0x21` and stays in report `0x3F`). `DM_HID_LOG=1` and `DM_HID_MAP` work as for any raw HID pad. The NANO reaches this path because GameController never lists it ([PR #859](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/859) reads pads of other vendors as raw HID).
+- **Fix (resolved):** buttons that come in report `0x3F` take that report's numbering, read from the device's elements and not from its name or vendor. `pad_hid_raw_axis` measures each of its axes from its first value and scales it by the reach seen so far, which starts at 0.7 and only grows: a full push reads 99 to 100%. The pad gets its player on its lights (`pad_hid_lights`: subcommand `0x30` of output report `0x01`; the NANO answers `80 30` in report `0x21` and stays in report `0x3F`). `DM_HID_LOG=1` and `DM_HID_MAP` work as for any raw HID pad. The NANO reaches this path because GameController never lists it ([PR #859](https://github.com/seedds/nxnative/pull/859) reads pads of other vendors as raw HID). Home is usage 13 in report `0x3F`, so holding it opens the quit prompt and positional A confirms.
 - **Limits:** report `0x3F` carries no motion, and this path sends the pad no rumble, so such a pad has no gyro or rumble here.
 - **Found on:** a PowerA NANO over Bluetooth on macOS 27.2; it is not specific to a game. Played in [FINAL FANTASY RESONANCE](../../dm/games/0100606026F12000/README.md): buttons, full-tilt running and the player light.
 
